<!-- ─── Section 6: Conclusions ─── -->
<section id="conclusions">
  <div class="section-title">
    <span class="section-number">6</span>
    Key Takeaways &amp; Outlook
  </div>
  <div class="section-divider"></div>
  <p class="section-lead">
    The following observations are drawn directly from catalog data and carry immediate implications
    for spectrum policy, orbital debris regulation, and constellation licensing frameworks.
  </p>

  <div class="card-grid-3">
    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-title" style="color:#1d4ed8;">LEO Congestion</div>
      <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--gray-700);line-height:1.65;">
        12,860 satellites — 86.5% of the active fleet — occupy LEO. The 2025 cohort alone (4,329 objects)
        exceeded what was achievable in all prior history. At current launch rates, LEO object density is
        approaching levels that intensify conjunction risk and elevate collision probability for all operators.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-title" style="color:#92400e;">Single-Operator Dominance</div>
      <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--gray-700);line-height:1.65;">
        Starlink's 10,083 satellites (67.8%) mean that virtually every orbital-environment statistic is
        effectively a Starlink statistic. This concentration has significant implications for spectrum coordination
        — the Ku/Ka bands allocated to Starlink now represent the most congested slice of the radio spectrum in LEO.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-title" style="color:#065f46;">New Entrant Pressure</div>
      <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--gray-700);line-height:1.65;">
        Amazon Kuiper (210 satellites, rapid growth) and continued OneWeb operations (651) indicate the
        multi-constellation era is solidifying. Licensing frameworks that were designed for tens or hundreds
        of objects per operator require fundamental revision to accommodate systems authorised for thousands.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-title" style="color:#9d174d;">Orbit Circularity</div>
      <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--gray-700);line-height:1.65;">
        92.1% of active satellites maintain near-circular orbits (eccentricity &lt;0.001). This concentration
        in narrow altitude bands amplifies debris risk: a single collision event in a high-density shell could
        generate fragments at the exact orbital altitude of thousands of other operational satellites.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-title" style="color:#065f46;">Recency of Active Fleet</div>
      <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--gray-700);line-height:1.65;">
        90.5% of active satellites were launched since 2020. This recency reduces average on-orbit age,
        which is positive for reliability — but also reflects how rapidly operators must deorbit earlier
        generations to stay within ITU coordination agreements and avoid adding to the resident space object count.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <div class="card-title" style="color:#1d4ed8;">Policy Implications</div>
      <p style="font-size:14px;color:var(--gray-700);line-height:1.65;">
        The 5-year growth from 1,414 to 14,875 active satellites requires that debris-mitigation rules
        (deorbit timelines, passivation requirements), spectrum coordination processes, and conjunction
        screening capacity scale proportionally. Current ITU and national regulatory frameworks were not
        designed for this velocity of deployment.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

